@ 36.3.1.3 - Proto-Naish to Naxi
# citation: Pogostick Man, from Jacques, Guillaume, and Alexis Michaud (2011), “Approaching the historical phonology of three highly eroded Sino-Tibetan languages: Naxi, Na and Laze”. Diachronica 28:4 (2011), 468 – 498
	a iN {i,u} > e ɚɯ / P:[-voice]_%
	a > i / ŋ_%
	a > ɯ / :{ S_%, w_% }:
	a > wa / {[+ cons, - fr, + bk, + hi, - lo],ŋ}w_%
	a > e // [+ cons, - fr, + bk, + hi, - lo]_%
	{ a, aS } > { ɑ, {ɑ,o} } / _%
	iN > ɚ / {P,C}r_%
	i > ɯ / :{ S_%, kr_% }:
	u > ɚ / Pr_%
	{ u, o } > { v̩, u }
	[+back] > v̩ / :{ qʰ_, (ŋ)q_ }:
	V% > mid tone / C_ru
	V% > high tone
	N > ∅ / _pV
	{ {r,s}pʰ, {r,s}(N)p, {r,s}b, {r,s}mb } > { pʰ, p, b, mb } / _V (the paper implies similar development͡s occurred with stops at other PO:[+affricate]s)
	{r,s}(N)k > k / _V
	{ Sb, Sk, Sɡ } > { b, ?, ɡ } / _V
	n̥ > l̥ / %_V
	l̥ > h / %_V
	{r,s} > ∅ / %_V
	{r,s}N > N̥
	N̥ > hṼ
	hṼ > hV